Building the dimensions with surrogate keys.

The bill's attribute fields become the dimension tables. You group the A fields by the entity they describe, and each group is a dimension — fitted with a surrogate key, its natural key kept, and an SCD policy chosen per column.

So, group the attributes into dimensions. The customer id, name, and shipping and billing address go into dim customer. The product id, name, price, line, and category go into dim product. The order date goes into dim date. The channel goes into dim channel. And the promo code goes into dim promotion.

Now fit each dimension out, using the module-four checklist. Add a surrogate key — customer key, product key, and so on — the integer the fact will point at. Keep the natural key as an attribute — customer i-d, product i-d — for source traceability. Choose SCD per attribute — the customer address needs history, so Type 2; a corrected name is Type 1; that's module six. And reuse the conformed version — dim date and dim customer already exist enterprise-wide, so point at them rather than rebuild.

So dim customer, built, looks like this: a customer key as the primary, surrogate key; the customer i-d as the natural key from the bill; then name, segment, city, province, region, country; and the SCD-2 control columns — effective date, expiry date, and is-current.

And here's why the surrogate matters at this exact step. The bill only knows the natural key — C-four-four-seven-one. The dimension mints a surrogate — eleven-oh-one — so the fact can join on a fast integer and carry history. Ana's address change becomes a second row with a new customer key, and old order lines still point at the old version; that's module six. The surrogate is what lets one bill's customer become a versioned dimension row. So: group the bill's attributes by entity into dimension tables, each with a surrogate key, its natural key retained, and a per-attribute SCD policy — reusing conformed dimensions wherever they already exist.
